          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When a reference note names a root like K21, what do I look for to get to the specific code?",
          "answer": "The search results explain how to read the notes around a code, but they don't show how to get from K21 to a full code. They don't list the more specific codes under K21 or say how to choose one, so I can't give you that step.\n\nThe sources do describe what to watch for:\n\n- **Category context.** K21 is gastro-esophageal reflux disease, placed after esophagitis in the digestive chapter (*Chapter 11: diseases of the digestive system*, and *Coding gastroesophageal reflux disease: K21*).\n- **Notes that change the choice.** Notes such as Excludes1 (the two conditions can't both be coded) and Excludes2 (both can be coded) affect which code applies. \"Code first\" and \"use additional code\" set the order. Combination codes can replace separate codes (*ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*).\n- **Other codes that can apply.** The K21 document says hiatal hernia, dysphagia and some other conditions are coded separately. Symptoms are coded from the symptom chapter only when no diagnosis is documented.\n\nTo find the specific code, check the full ICD-10-CM tabular list and alphabetic index for K21, or ask a medical coder. If you can share the exact wording of the reference note, I can check which of these conventions it touches.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
